The film plays the page before any cell exists, then the resting grid, then one cell changing color while every square stays where it was put. Export video saves one pass. The stage under the film is that resting grid.

The picture is a field of squares. I kept having to say that again, because a diamond wants to be drawn as a rotated square, or as two diagonals, or as an outline. None of those are this. The squares are laid down once. The diamond, later, is only a decision about which of them are bright.

STEP 1 — THE PAGE IS ALREADY THE BLUE

The artboard is a portrait, 1650 by 2550. A page that tall, with overflow hidden and no scaling, shows the top margin and hides the center below the window. I draw into a canvas of that size, then scale the canvas with CSS so the whole artboard fits the window. The drawing coordinates stay 1650 by 2550. The transform is identity. One bitmap pixel is one artboard pixel.

```
export const VIEW_W = 1650;
export const VIEW_H = 2550;

canvas.width = viewW;
canvas.height = viewH;
const scale = Math.min(window.innerWidth / viewW, window.innerHeight / viewH);
canvas.style.width = `${Math.round(viewW * scale)}px`;
canvas.style.height = `${Math.round(viewH * scale)}px`;
ctx.setTransform(1, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0);
```

The page background is the same blue as the field, #1C1CD1, so the margin around the scaled canvas does not introduce a second color. The body is a grid with one cell, centered. Nothing scrolls.

STEP 2 — AN ODD COUNT, SO THERE IS ONE CENTER

A diamond that starts at “the center” needs a center cell, not a point between four cells. Columns and rows are forced odd. The target cell is about 26 pixels. Width decides the column count and the cell size. Height decides how many rows fit. At 1650 by 2550 that lands on 59 by 95, a 24 pixel square, and a 2 pixel gap. The center cell is column 29, row 47.

```
const margin = 40;
const gap = 2;
let cols = Math.round(innerW / 26);
if (cols % 2 === 0) cols -= 1;
const cell = Math.floor((innerW - gap * (cols - 1)) / cols);
let rows = Math.floor((innerH + gap) / (cell + gap));
if (rows % 2 === 0) rows -= 1;
```

The gap is a hairline of the field showing between squares. It is the grid. It is not a hole in the diamond. Activated neighbors stay adjacent squares. They do not grow to swallow the line, and they do not leave a rectangular pocket of inactive cells inside the wave.

STEP 3 — THE SQUARE IS STORED ONCE

Each cell remembers where it sits and how far it is from the center. The distance is computed when the grid is built, not every frame. A resize rebuilds the list. A frame only reads it.

```
cells[i] = {
  x: ox + col * (cell + gap),
  y: oy + row * (cell + gap),
  col,
  row,
  distance: manhattan(col, row, cx, cy),
};
```

x and y never change during the pulse. There is no scale, no rotation, no offset that walks a square across the field. The animation is a color. Resting, then bright, then resting again.

STEP 4 — THE RESTING GRID HAS TO BE VISIBLE

If the idle squares use the same blue as the field, the page looks empty. I mix the resting squares from purple toward blue, a little lighter than the field, so the grid reads before anything has started. The field itself stays in the two darker colors, blue toward a little purple, and breathes slowly. It does not compete with the wave.

```
export function restingColor(time) {
  const shift = 0.5 - 0.5 * Math.cos(time * 0.16);
  return mixRgb(PURPLE, BLUE, 0.22 + shift * 0.12);
}
```

On this page the stage is that state: every square present, none of them in the wave. The next page is the rule that decides the diamond.

The film plays one pulse: center, ring, dark center, edge, then the resting grid. The stage does the same. Click it, or press Space, to play it again. It does not repeat by itself.

A filled diamond grows and then sits there, lit. I did not want the cells behind the front to stay lit. I wanted the diamond itself to be what moves. The center is bright, then the center is the grid again, and a band of squares is farther out.

STEP 1 — BRIGHTNESS IS DISTANCE FROM THE FRONT

Every cell still has a Manhattan distance. That distance is compared to the current radius, which is where the front is, in cell steps. The difference is delta. Zero is the front. Positive is still ahead of the wave, in cells the ring has not reached. Negative is behind it, in cells the ring has already passed.

```
export const LEAD = 2.2;
export const TAIL = 5.5;

export function cellAmount(distance, radius) {
  const delta = distance - radius;
  if (delta >= LEAD || delta <= -TAIL) return 0;
  if (delta >= 0) return smoothstep(1 - delta / LEAD);
  return smoothstep(1 + delta / TAIL);
}
```

Ahead of the front, the fade is 2.2 cells. Behind it, the fade is 5.5 cells. Outside that window the cell is fully resting. The peak is the front, delta === 0, where the amount is 1. Smoothstep softens both shoulders. Cells at the same distance get the same amount, so a ring cannot develop a hole, a stripe, or a missing side. The band is every adjacent square whose distance is close to the radius.

STEP 2 — THE CENTER GOES DARK BECAUSE THE FRONT LEAVES

At the start, progress is 0 and the radius is 0. The center cell is the front, so it is fully bright, and the cells within the lead are the soft edge of a small diamond. As the radius grows, the center’s delta becomes more negative. Once the radius has passed the tail, 5.5 cells, the center’s amount is 0. It is back in the resting color while the ring is still traveling.

```
export function radiusFor(progress, maxD) {
  if (progress <= 0) return 0;
  return easeInOutCubic(progress) * (maxD + TAIL);
}
```

The extra TAIL past maxD is so the last cells, the corners, get to fade out before the clock hits 1. If the radius stopped on the farthest cell, those corners would still be the bright front when the animation ended. Adding the tail means progress 1 is a dark grid.

STEP 3 — THE RING, READ AS CHARACTERS

Same function, printed. X is the front, above 0.72. + is the shoulder. · is resting. This frame is progress 0.4 on a 21 by 21 grid. The middle of the middle row is dark. The bright cells are on either side of it. That is the ring crossing one row twice.

STEP 4 — THE ORDER OF THE PULSE

Center first. The diamond of the front expands. The center falls back through the tail into the resting grid. The ring keeps going. On the portrait artboard the left and right tips reach the side edges before the top and bottom, because those edges are fewer steps away. The ring stays a diamond of cells the whole way. When the front has passed the last corner, and the tail has faded off it, the field is the resting grid again.

The film plays a linear ring beside an eased ring, then leaves both of them finished. The stage does that pair once. Click either side, or press Space, to run the five seconds again. Escape clears them. Neither one starts the next pass by itself.

The wave has a path. It also has to take five seconds to walk it, slow at the start, faster through the middle, slow again at the edge, and then be over. A summed frame delta made that fragile. A stalled frame either froze the picture or, if I had not capped it, would have skipped the pulse.

STEP 1 — FIVE SECONDS, NAMED ONCE

The duration is one constant, in milliseconds. Changing the pulse means changing that number. Progress is elapsed divided by it. At 0 the radius is 0 and the center is the front. At 1 the radius is the farthest cell plus the tail, and every cell is resting.

```
const ANIMATION_DURATION = 5000;

elapsed = now - startedAt;
if (elapsed >= ANIMATION_DURATION) {
  elapsed = ANIMATION_DURATION;
  mode = 'done';
}
const progress = elapsed / ANIMATION_DURATION;
```

Elapsed is wall time, performance.now() minus the moment Space was pressed. It is not a pile of frame lengths. If a frame arrives late, the ring is wherever five seconds of the clock says it should be. It does not creep forward 50 milliseconds per stalled frame, and it does not jump to the end because one delta was huge.

STEP 2 — EASE-IN-OUT, STILL EXACTLY FIVE SECONDS

The radius is the eased progress, not the raw progress. Cubic ease-in-out is slow, then fast, then slow, and it is 0 at the start and 1 at the end. The five seconds do not change. Only how the radius spends them changes. On the artboard the front reaches the side edges a little after two seconds, the top and bottom just after that, and the corners around three and a half seconds. The last stretch is the tail leaving those corners. At five seconds the grid is dark and stays dark.

```
export function easeInOutCubic(t) {
  const x = clamp(t, 0, 1);
  return x < 0.5 ? 4 * x * x * x : 1 - ((-2 * x + 2) ** 3) / 2;
}
```

The left grid on the stage ignores that curve. Its radius is progress * (maxD + tail). The right grid is the piece. They start together and finish together. Through the middle, the right one is ahead, then it waits for the left one at the end.

STEP 3 — ONE PLAY, AND THE KEYS

The mode is idle, playing, or done. Idle draws every cell resting, with a radius sent far enough negative that even the center is outside the lead. Playing stores startedAt and draws from the clock. Done holds progress at 1, which is also a resting picture, and stays there. Space while playing returns immediately, so a second press does not restart the ring from the center halfway through. Space after done, or from idle, starts a new pulse at the center. Escape sets idle and draws the resting grid on that same turn.

```
function start() {
  if (mode === 'playing') return;
  startedAt = performance.now();
  mode = 'playing';
  paint(startedAt);
}

function reset() {
  mode = 'idle';
  startedAt = 0;
  paint(performance.now());
}
```

Space is event.code === 'Space', or the key ' ', or the older 'Spacebar'. Escape is either event.key or event.code. Both calls prevent the browser from scrolling the page. A pointer down on the canvas focuses it and starts the same function, because a key never arrives if the page is not what the browser is typing into.

STEP 4 — THE FRAME THAT NEVER CAME BACK

The first version asked for the next frame at the end of frame, and only there. The first paint ran, because it was called directly at startup. Then, in the embedded browser, requestAnimationFrame did not fire again. Space set mode to playing. The canvas kept the idle grid. The animation had started and the picture had not moved. I had already watched a version of this: a negative remainder killed the loop, and the page sat on the body color. This time the loop did not throw. It just was not called.

```
function tick() {
  const now = performance.now();
  if (now - lastPaint < 12) return;
  lastPaint = now;
  paint(now);
}

requestAnimationFrame(function loop() {
  tick();
  requestAnimationFrame(loop);
});
setInterval(tick, 16);
```

start also paints immediately, so the center lights on the key itself, before the next callback. The interval is the backup clock. The 12 millisecond guard keeps the two schedulers from drawing twice in the same instant. Idle, before any key, is still idle. The interval paints the resting grid. It does not start the pulse.

The film holds a diamond colored from corner to corner, which reads as a sweep, beside the same diamond colored from the center. Then it plays the ring in the coloring I kept. The stage shows both diamonds at rest. Click it, or press Space, and both play the ring once: corner coloring on the left, center coloring on the right.

There are four colors. Everything else is a mix of neighbors in that list. No fifth hue, and no color chosen per cell at random. The field has to stay quiet. The wave has to stay obvious on top of it.

STEP 1 — THE FOUR STOPS

```
const BLUE = [28, 28, 209];
const PURPLE = [101, 14, 114];
const MAGENTA = [248, 2, 137];
const YELLOW = [223, 255, 13];
const PALETTE = [BLUE, PURPLE, MAGENTA, YELLOW];
```

paletteAt walks that list. The position is scaled by 3, because there are three steps between four stops. Smoothstep eases each step so the join is not a hard band. Exact 1 used to fall through 1 % 1 === 0 and come back as blue. A positive integer maps to the last stop, yellow, and stays there.

STEP 2 — THE FIELD AND THE RESTING SQUARE

The background mixes blue toward purple, between about 0.08 and 0.20, on a slow cosine. The resting square mixes purple toward blue, between about 0.22 and 0.34, on the same cosine. Both stay in the dark pair. The square is purple enough to read as a cell. The field is blue enough to read as the ground. Neither one reaches magenta or yellow. Those two are reserved for the wave.

```
export function backgroundColor(time) {
  const shift = 0.5 - 0.5 * Math.cos(time * 0.16);
  return mixRgb(BLUE, PURPLE, 0.08 + shift * 0.12);
}
```

STEP 3 — A CORNER GRADIENT HID HALF THE DIAMOND

I colored activated cells by where they sat on the page, column plus row, from blue in one corner to yellow in the other. The diamond was a real diamond in the distances. On the blue and purple side it was the same color as the field, so the eye kept the yellow half and threw the rest away. It looked like a wipe from corner to corner. The left side of the stage’s film is that mistake, frozen on a filled diamond so the missing half is obvious.

```
const n = manhattan(col, row, cx, cy) / Math.max(1, cx + cy);
const ring = paletteAt(1 - n * 0.7);
const glow = mixRgb(YELLOW, MAGENTA, 0.25 + n * 0.45 + drift * 0.2);
return mixRgb(ring, glow, 0.7);
```

The color I kept follows the same center as the diamond. n is 0 at the center and 1 at the farthest cell. 1 - n * 0.7 starts at yellow and only walks part of the way back down the palette, so the edge of the ring is still magenta, not blue. That result is mixed 70 percent toward a glow of yellow and magenta. A slow cosine drifts the glow. A lit cell does not camouflage. The center of the ring is the hottest yellow. Farther rings cool toward magenta. The resting mix from the previous step is what colorFor returns to when the amount is 0.

```
export function colorFor(amount, background, active) {
  return mixRgb(background, active, smoothstep(clamp(amount, 0, 1)));
}
```

Amount is already a smoothstep inside cellAmount. Smoothing it again pulls the shoulders in, so the bright core stays narrow and the tail dissolves into the purple grid instead of ending in a hard cut. The front is still full yellow where the amount is 1.

STEP 4 — WHAT THE PASS IS, TOGETHER

Space, or a click on the canvas. The center cell lights yellow. The ring moves out under the ease, magenta cooling in as the radius grows. Cells behind the tail return to the resting purple. At five seconds every cell is resting, the mode is done, and the interval keeps painting that still grid. Space from there starts at the center again. Escape does it immediately, from any moment. The squares are in the same places they were in step 1 of the grid page.

The film plays the iterations of this step, in order, and loops. Export video records one pass at 1920×1080. Quiet differences are lifted just enough to survive compression. The settled field under the film is the picture I kept.

I started from the TouchDesigner field: a rectangle of isometric cubes, some filled, some outlined, pastel faces on a saturated ground. No buttons, no sliders, no menus. I knew that picture. I did not yet know how a browser page holds one. The page had to become the picture, and almost every line on this page is something I learned because the browser did something I had not asked for.

STEP 1 — THE PAGE IS THE FIELD

A new html file does not open onto a blank stage. It opens onto the browser’s page: a white margin, and room to scroll. I had not drawn a cube yet. I cleared that first, and I set the background to the green of the first scene, so the empty page was already the field.

```
html, body {
  margin: 0;
  height: 100%;
  background: #3ad24c;
  color-scheme: only light;
  overflow: hidden;
}
```

I had to look up what each of these actually does. Margin 0 is the white gap. Without it the canvas sits inside a frame I did not design. Height 100 percent is what gives the canvas a real box. Before that, the drawing surface had no height and I thought the draw call was broken. Overflow hidden comes later, once there is a sentence. A long sentence was stretching the page and bringing the scrollbar back. I hid the overflow so the page cannot grow.

The green I understood as a backdrop. I only learned its second job when something crashed. The drawing stops, the canvas stays empty, and this green is all that is left. A flat green field became my way of seeing that the frame had died. I will need that reading twice more.

STEP 2 — THE FIRST CUBES WERE THE WRONG SHAPE

I thought any view from above-and-to-the-side would give me a cube. I aimed the camera along (1, −1, 1). The grid was there. The faces came back as chevrons. I did not yet see that the minus on that middle axis folds the top of the cube into an arrow. The film’s first iteration is that mistake, drawn large enough to read. I changed the view to (1, 1, 1). Then I had to learn the two lines that actually put a point on the screen. A cube in this projection lands on a hexagon. I checked the silhouette before I trusted it.

```
const INV_SQRT2 = 1 / Math.sqrt(2);
const INV_SQRT6 = 1 / Math.sqrt(6);

function projectPoint(v, cubeSize) {
  return [
    (v[0] - v[1]) * INV_SQRT2 * cubeSize,
    (v[0] + v[1] - 2 * v[2]) * INV_SQRT6 * cubeSize,
  ];
}
```

I had to sit with the formula before it meant anything. x and y are the two ground directions. z is up. The first line, x minus y, is the screen’s horizontal. That difference is why the cube leans instead of sitting square to the window. The second line adds x and y, because both of those directions fall away from me, and takes z off, because up on the cube has to be up on the screen. INV_SQRT2 and INV_SQRT6 are the scaling that keeps those directions equal. I multiply by the cube’s size only after that, so making a cube bigger does not squash it. The filled-and-outlined checker I already had from the TouchDesigner patch: an even column-plus-row is filled, an odd one is a line drawing. That was a decision I brought with me. The projection was the part I got wrong.

STEP 3 — CHROME WAS BLACK, THE PREVIEW WAS GREEN

I opened the same files in Chrome and the page was black. In the editor’s preview the field was green and the cubes were there. I spent a while believing I had broken the draw code between the two views. I had not changed it. Chrome was repainting the page into a dark scheme on its own. I had never had to tell a browser that a color was already the color I meant.

```
<meta name="color-scheme" content="only light">
```

color-scheme is a note to the browser about whether it is allowed to recolor the document. only light means leave it alone. I put the same words in the CSS as well, because the tag in the head and the stylesheet were two separate instructions until I repeated it. A normal reload was not enough. The black version was cached. Command-Shift-R showed the field. I left the line on every page after this, these notes included, because I had learned the black was the browser, not the cubes.

STEP 4 — AN OPAQUE CANVAS ALSO WENT BLACK

Still chasing the black, I tried to speed the canvas up. I had read that passing alpha: false makes the surface opaque and skips a blending step. On this machine that context came back black, including when I filled it with the same green that worked everywhere else. The pixels were green. The compositor threw the surface out. I stopped chasing the speed.

```
const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
```

getContext('2d') with nothing after it is the ordinary surface, the one that still has an alpha channel. That is the line I kept. I do not pass alpha: false. A fast canvas I cannot see is not faster.

STEP 5 — SHARP ON A RETINA SCREEN, STILL DRAWN IN CSS PIXELS

Once the field was actually green, the cube edges looked soft. I thought the line width was wrong. It was the screen. On the laptop in front of me, one CSS pixel is two physical pixels. If the canvas bitmap is only as wide as the window, the browser stretches it and the edges blur. If I redo every coordinate in physical pixels, the grid has to know which screen it is on. I did not want the sculpture’s math to contain the monitor. I split them.

```
dpr = Math.min(window.devicePixelRatio || 1, 2);
canvas.width = Math.round(viewW * dpr);
canvas.height = Math.round(viewH * dpr);
ctx.setTransform(dpr, 0, 0, dpr, 0, 0);
```

devicePixelRatio is the number I did not have a name for. Here it is 2. I cap it at 2 because I do not yet know what a denser screen would cost in pixels, and I would rather the picture stay light. canvas.width and canvas.height are the bitmap, so they get the window size times that ratio. The CSS size of the canvas stays the window, or the bitmap and the layout stop matching and I get a scrollbar again. setTransform(dpr, 0, 0, dpr, 0, 0) scales the pen. I had to try this a few times before I believed it. A cube I place at x = 100 is still 100 CSS pixels from the left. On this screen the pen turns that into 200 device pixels. The grid never hears the word density.

At the end of this step I have a field of cubes, filled and outlined, filling the window, and a short list of ways I was wrong about a page. Nothing answers a key yet. The next thing I wanted was to play it by typing, and I still thought typing meant a text box.

The film types a sentence on its own, so the iteration can be recorded without anyone at the keyboard. Export video saves one pass. The plate under the film is the same instrument, live, if you want to play it.

The field from the previous step could be poked with number keys. That was me checking behaviors, not playing the piece. I took those keys out before I had anything to replace them with, which I regretted a day later. What I wanted was to play it by writing a sentence, and to have the sentence stay on the picture. I did not yet know that a page can hear the keyboard without a text box.

STEP 1 — THE SENTENCE IS NOT A FORM

I reached for an input, because that is how every page I know takes typing. It gave me a caret, a focus ring, and the look of a form sitting on the picture. I took it out. What I wanted was a small plate in the upper right, something that belongs to the image, and that is absent until the first character. I had to find out which element would hold a sentence without becoming a form.

```
<pre id="transcript"></pre>

#transcript:empty { display: none; }

insert(char) {
  value += char;
  element.textContent = value;
  element.scrollTop = element.scrollHeight;
}
```

A normal element ate my spaces. That is why it is a pre: it keeps the spaces and the line breaks as I type them. I had innerHTML first, and I realized a typed < would be read as markup, so the string goes in through textContent, which writes characters and does not parse them. :empty was a surprise. It is a CSS state for an element with nothing inside it. When the string is empty the whole plate, border included, is display none, so the rectangle is not sitting on the field waiting. scrollTop I added once the sentence got longer than the box and the new letters were happening underneath the edge. The plate is fixed to the upper right. Its background is only partly opaque, because a solid box would have cut a hole in the picture.

STEP 2 — ONE FUNCTION ANSWERS FOR EVERY KEY

My first version was going to be a chain of ifs inside the animation. I could already see I would lose which letter did what. I pulled the letters out into one table. The rest of the piece is only allowed to ask one question: what does this key mean? I can retune a gesture later without hunting through the drawing.

```
const groups = [
  { characters: 'aeiou', behavior: 'swell' },
  { characters: 'bcdgkpqt', behavior: 'flip' },
  { characters: 'mnlr', behavior: 'pulse' },
  { characters: 'fhsvxzj', behavior: 'scatter' },
  { characters: 'wy', behavior: 'shift' },
  {
    characters: '1234567890',
    behaviors: ['swell','flip','pulse','scatter','shift','rotate','swell','flip','pulse','scatter'],
  },
];
```

The groups are a guess I am willing to revise. Vowels swell. A cluster of consonants flips. Another pulses, another scatters, and w and y shift the columns. I grouped them by how the gesture felt next to the letter, not by a system I can fully defend yet. The useful part is that a whole class lives in one string, so moving h from scatter to shift is one character, not a search. The number row I kept as a second keyboard, because I still wanted to fire one behavior on purpose. The behaviors list has to stay in the same order as the characters. I had them drift once, and 4 was no longer scatter. 1 swells. 6 rotates. 0 is the last one, scatter again.

A capital missed its group. The table is lowercase, and I was searching it with the key as typed. The sentence still stores event.key unchanged, so the plate can hold a capital. The lookup uses a lowercased copy. Two uses of the same key. I had been treating them as one.

STEP 3 — FOUR KEYS THAT ARE NOT LETTERS

I did not plan these four at the start. They showed up one at a time, in the order the sentence demanded them.

```
' ':      { type: 'insert', char: ' ', behavior: null, sound: null }
Enter:    { type: 'newline', behavior: 'rotate', sound: { notes: ['c3','g3'] } }
Escape:   { type: 'reset', behavior: 'reset', sound: null }
Backspace:{ type: 'backspace', behavior: null, sound: { s: 'square', note: 'c6' } }
```

Space came first, because a sentence without spaces is a block. The character is inserted. behavior null is me learning that “do nothing to the grid” has to be written down. If I leave the field off, the code goes looking for a behavior that is not there. Space is also the key the browser uses to scroll. I did not want that key to be a visual event on top of being a scroll event. The scroll problem is the next step. Here, the decision is only that the word gets a gap and the cubes stay put.

Enter felt like a turn in the sentence, so I gave it the gesture that turns the whole field. The grid rotates. The two notes do not exist yet. I am leaving a slot for them. At this point I only know Enter should be larger than a letter.

Then I had no way to put the field back. I had deleted the test keys, and I was sitting in front of a scattered grid. Escape was the key I had left. It clears the sentence and tells the grid to unwind toward home. I left it silent. I wanted the reset to be something I see.

Backspace should remove the last character and leave the cubes alone. The little click comes in the sound step. The delete itself took a wrong turn first:

```
value = Array.from(value).slice(0, -1).join('');
```

I used slice. It counts code units, and a character can be more than one unit. A delete ate the wrong amount. Array.from splits the string into characters, slice drops the last of those, and join puts the string back. One keypress, one glyph. I did not know a string was stored that way until the backspace misbehaved.

STEP 4 — THE BROWSER WAS PLAYING TOO

The first time I typed a sentence, the page scrolled. Space jumped the view. The arrows scrolled. I had thought the page was mine once it was full-screen. The browser was listening to the same keys. I added the guards in the order I hit the problems, and the first fix broke something else.

```
window.addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {
  if (event.metaKey || event.ctrlKey || event.altKey || event.isComposing) return;

  const resolved = eventForKey(event.key);
  if (!resolved) {
    if (scrollKeys.has(event.key)) event.preventDefault();
    return;
  }

  event.preventDefault();
  applyEvent(resolved);
});
```

I put preventDefault on every key, and copy, paste, and screenshots died. Command, Control, and Alt have to leave the function before that line. I returned early for them. isComposing I found when holding an accent fired a behavior for a character I had not finished. The browser was reporting the halfway key. Then preventDefault stays on every key the map understands, so the browser does not scroll and does not type into some other focused control. scrollKeys is the leftover set, the keys the map has nothing to say about and the page still must not scroll for: arrows, page up and down, home, end, tab, space, backspace.

The listener is on window. I kept looking for the box the typing goes into. There is no box. The page itself is the listener. That took me longer to believe than the table of letters did.

STEP 5 — WHAT A RESOLVED KEY RETURNS

```
return {
  type: 'insert',
  char: key,
  behavior: nextBehavior,
  sound: soundFor(nextBehavior, lower, extra),
};
```

I split the answer into four fields before I had a sound to put in the last one. type tells the sentence what to do: insert, a new line, backspace, or reset. char is the glyph, with its capital intact. behavior is the name the grid already knew from the test keyboard: swell, flip, pulse, scatter, shift, rotate. sound is empty for now. At the time the empty field felt like unfinished work. It is what let me finish the sentence and the grid before the synth existed. That mattered more than I knew. The synth was about to take the picture down with it.

The film walks the glass iterations: the clean picture, the gray veil from the overlay blend, then grain and scanlines, then the sentence still readable under them. Grain and lines are a little stronger here than in the piece, so they survive a recording. Export video saves one pass.

The picture, the sentence, and the sound were already the piece. I knew I wanted them seen through an old screen. I did not know how to age a picture without the picture becoming the aging. If I bent the cubes, the sentence I had just gotten readable would bend too. So this step is me learning to put a screen in front of a picture, and checking, after each try, that I had not gone back into the behavior code.

STEP 1 — THREE LAYERS, IN THIS ORDER

My first thought was to warp the cubes while I drew them. I stopped, because that warp would be part of the sculpture, and the type would warp with it. I wanted the letters readable, and I still wanted them in the same glass as the cubes. I did not have a structure for that. It ended up as three things, in this order: the picture, then the sentence, then a full-screen canvas that must not receive clicks.

```
<canvas id="stage"></canvas>
<pre id="transcript"></pre>
