z-index I understood as stacking order only after the type covered the scanlines and looked like it was sitting in front of the screen. The sentence was already at 3. The glass had to be 4. inset 0 stretches it to every edge. position fixed keeps it from scrolling, which mattered because I had already watched this page jump. pointer-events none is the line I almost forgot. Without it the glass canvas becomes a target. The keys still worked, because they listen on the window, and a click died, and I could not tell why those two disagreed. The glass is not an interface. It has to be unable to become one.

I learned I can make a canvas that is not in the page at all. The grid draws there, clean. What I see is a copy. The glass is painted on top of the copy. When I turn the treatment off, I copy the clean bitmap and erase the glass. I was relieved. I did not have to un-age the cubes. They never knew they were being shown on an old display.

STEP 2 — A SWITCH THAT IS NOT A SLIDER

I was going to add a slider, because that is how I tune things. A slider would have turned the piece back into a page with a control on it. I put the numbers in one object instead. Hanging it on window is a trick I had just learned from the sound import: a value on window can be typed at from the console while the piece is running. displaySettings.intensity = 0.5, and I can see it. No reload, no menu.

```
const displaySettings = {
  enabled: true,
  intensity: 1,
  noise: 1,
  scanlines: 1,
  bars: 1,
  distortion: 1,
  chroma: 1,
  flicker: 1,
};
window.displaySettings = displaySettings;

function level(name) {
  return displaySettings[name] * displaySettings.intensity;
}
```

I did not get the scaling right immediately. level multiplies one named trim by the master, once. intensity 0.5 with noise 1 is half the grain. noise 0 removes the grain and leaves the scanlines. I state it this carefully because in the next step I multiplied the same number twice and then could not understand why the knob felt broken. Enabled false, or intensity at 0, takes the early path: copy the clean picture, clear the glass, stop. I wanted to be able to see the piece without the screen, to check I had not damaged the picture while I was aging it.

STEP 3 — THE FIRST GRAIN WAS A VEIL

I wanted fine noise, changing every frame, across the whole screen, sentence included. I looked up a blend mode called overlay because the name sounded like dust on a picture. The glass canvas is transparent. Overlay mixes with whatever is behind the pixel, and behind these pixels is transparency, which behaved like a flat gray. The field went milky. The cubes lost the contrast I had spent the first step getting. I had gone looking for a special mode. The ordinary composite, the default, was the one that looked like dust. I took the blend off and drew speckles that carry their own alpha.

```
for (let i = 0; i < pixels.length; i += 4) {
  const value = Math.random() < 0.5 ? 255 : 0;
  pixels[i] = pixels[i + 1] = pixels[i + 2] = value;
  pixels[i + 3] = 70 + ((Math.random() * 90) | 0);
}
noiseCtx.putImageData(noiseImage, 0, 0);

glass.globalAlpha = 0.14 * grain;
glass.fillStyle = glass.createPattern(noiseCanvas, 'repeat');
const ox = Math.random() * 128;
const oy = Math.random() * 128;
glass.translate(-ox, -oy);
glass.fillRect(ox, oy, pixelW + 128, pixelH + 128);
```

I tried to randomize a full-screen buffer and the frame stumbled. 128 by 128 is the size I could rewrite every frame and then tile. ImageData is a flat list I had to learn by printing its length. Four numbers per pixel: red, green, blue, alpha. i += 4 is one pixel. I put the same number in the three color slots so a speck is black or white. A colored speck tinted the cubes, and I did not want the grain to have a palette of its own. The alpha of each speck is a random number from 70 to 160, out of 255. I arrived there by the grain being either invisible or a fog. 0.14 on the whole tile is a second fade, after each speck is already soft. putImageData writes the list back onto the tiny canvas. createPattern is what repeats it. When the tile sat still I could see the seam, a grid inside the noise. A new random offset each frame slides the tile. The first time I translated it, a gap opened at the edge, so the filled rectangle is one tile larger than the screen. The composite is source-over, which is just the default I had tried to replace. The cubes stay at full contrast, with dust on the glass.

STEP 4 — SCANLINES GOT THICK ON A RETINA SCREEN

I made the scanlines with a CSS gradient, because it was one line and it looked right in the editor. On my screen they were stripes. I had just learned, from the soft cube edges, that a CSS pixel here is two physical pixels. The gradient was measured in CSS pixels, so the hairline was two device pixels wide and the field looked ruled, like a graphic I had drawn on purpose. I wanted one physical pixel, and I did not yet know how to draw in that unit.

```
scanCanvas.width = 1;
scanCanvas.height = 3;
scanCtx.fillStyle = 'rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.85)';
scanCtx.fillRect(0, 2, 1, 1);

glass.setTransform(1, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0);
glass.globalAlpha = 0.16 * scan;
glass.fillStyle = glass.createPattern(scanCanvas, 'repeat');
glass.fillRect(0, 0, pixelW, pixelH);
```

The tile is one bitmap pixel wide and three tall. I fill only the last row. Two rows stay clear, one is dark, and the pattern repeats. My lines were still thick after this, which is how I found that the glass context was still scaled by dpr. My “one pixel” was being multiplied. setTransform(1, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0) puts the pen back into device pixels, the same space as pixelW, which is already the window times the ratio. Alpha 0.16 is where I stopped turning it down. Darker than that, the pastel faces went dull and I lost the cubes under the screen. The sentence is under this canvas, so the lines cross the letters. I read a sentence through them before I kept the number. The glyphs stay sharp because I never move the text. I only darken a hair of it.

Grain and scanlines were the standing layer. The next step is the things that happen only sometimes: a bar, a slip, a fringe, and the crash I found while testing them.

The film plays the unstable layer as separate iterations: a bar, the sideways slip, the color fringe, the frame dying on a bad index, then the field coming back. The slip and the fringe are stronger than in the piece so a compressed video still shows them. Export video saves one pass.

The glass from the previous step was steady: grain and scanlines, every frame. I wanted the screen to misbehave sometimes, and I did not yet know the difference between a misbehavior and an effect I had designed. A bar sometimes. A sideways slip sometimes. A little color, and only when the slip is actually moving. While I was testing it, the picture died again. Same flat green as the failed import. I assumed I had broken the glass. The cause was a different line, in the color clock, and I only found it because I already knew what a dead frame looks like.

STEP 1 — A BAR IS AN EVENT WITH A LIFETIME

I wanted long horizontal rectangles that interrupt the field and then leave. I spawned them with a coin flip every frame. On my machine that was a lot of bars. On a slower one there were fewer. I had made the chance per frame, so a faster computer was a different piece. The frames are not a clock. The seconds inside dt are the clock. I had to put dt into the probability before the rate meant anything.

```
if (bars.length < 2 && Math.random() < dt * 0.18 * amount) {
  const travel = Math.random() < 0.4;
  bars.push({
    y: Math.random() * viewH,
    h: 5 + Math.random() * 9,
    life: 0.45 + Math.random() * 0.85,
    travel,
    x: travel ? (Math.random() < 0.5 ? -0.85 : 1.15) : 0,
    width: travel ? 0.42 + Math.random() * 0.38 : 1,
    light: Math.random() < 0.55,
    drift: (Math.random() - 0.5) * 18,
  });
}
```

dt times 0.18 is about one birth every five or six seconds, and only while fewer than two bars exist. I capped the count after they started stacking into a solid band. Amount is the trim from the glass settings, so turning bars down stretches the wait. The rest of the numbers I found by the first bars being either invisible or huge. Height lands between 5 and 14 CSS pixels. Taller than that, a bar covered a cube and became a shape. Life is under a second and a half. A bar that stayed turned into a graphic element, which is the thing I was trying not to add. About 40 percent of them travel. They start offscreen. x is in screen-widths, not pixels, which I got wrong first: the bar appeared already in the middle, because -0.85 pixels is basically the left edge. -0.85 widths is actually offstage. Their width is 42 to 80 percent of the view. The other bars are full-width. Drift is a small vertical speed. Without it a bar sat on a scanline and I lost it.

```
const fade = Math.sin(Math.PI * Math.min(1, bar.age / bar.life));
glass.globalAlpha = fade * 0.32 * level('bars');
glass.fillRect(x, bar.y * dpr, width, bar.h * dpr);
```

A bar that popped on looked like a UI line. Age divided by life runs from 0 to 1. Sin of pi times that is 0 at the start, 1 in the middle, and 0 at the end, so I did not have to store a second fade. I delete from the list by walking backward. Forward, splice shifts the next index and I skip a bar. I found that by bars lingering after their life. Peak alpha 0.32 is as high as I could go and still see the cube through the rectangle. I tried a blur. It looked designed. I took it off. The edges stay hard.

STEP 2 — THE SLIP IS A COPY, NOT A NEW POSITION

I almost added the slip as another cube behavior. That would have moved the sculpture, and the behaviors were already doing that job. I wanted the monitor to slip, and the cubes to stay where they had been put. So I cut the finished picture into strips and redraw the strips a little to the side. The grid’s coordinates do not change. I did not know drawImage could take a source rectangle and a destination rectangle until I needed exactly a cut and a paste.

```
const SLICE = 6;
const shift = (wave * 0.85 + local * 2.4) * level('distortion');

viewCtx.drawImage(
  scene,
  0, y * dpr, viewW * dpr, sliceH * dpr,
  shift, y, viewW, sliceH,
);
```

I mixed the two coordinate spaces up, and the strips came out the wrong height, a few rows of the picture smeared down the screen. After the source, the first four numbers are the cut: x, y, width, height, in the bitmap’s own pixels. That is why y is multiplied by dpr. The last four are where it lands, in CSS pixels, because the pen is already scaled. Six pixels is the strip. Thinner was more draw calls, and I could feel it. Thicker stopped looking like a signal and started looking like blocks. The slow wave stays under a pixel. The burst is a sine raised to the 28th. I did not start with 28. A low power was a constant wobble. A high power stays near 0 until the sine is almost 1, so the jump is rare. It can reach about two pixels. Most of the time it is nothing.

The first version multiplied the distortion trim twice, once inside the shift and once outside it. I thought the intensity knob was broken. Half intensity was a quarter of the slip, because I was squaring it. I had written level() in both places and forgotten the first. It is applied once now.

```
const maxShift = (0.85 * Math.abs(envelope) + 2.4 * burst) * level('distortion');
if (maxShift > 0.55) {
```

Cutting up a retina frame every frame showed up as the machine working harder than the picture was moving. Most of the time the shift was under a pixel and I was still slicing the whole image to move it by nothing I could see. If the estimate is under about half a pixel, I leave the clean copy alone. The quiet picture is the usual case. The slip is the exception. I had built it as if the exception were the whole film.

STEP 3 — COLOR ON EVERY FRAME FELT LIKE A FILTER

I left a faint copy one pixel left and one pixel right, on every frame, because a little color error sounded like the brief. It was always on. The cubes looked coated. I recognized the look as a filter I could have dropped on any video, and I had been trying not to arrive there. The color had to show up with the slip, the way a signal fringes when it is already failing, and be gone when the picture is holding.

```
if (fringe > 0.01 && (Math.abs(envelope) > 0.72 || burst > 0.15)) {
  viewCtx.globalAlpha = 0.045 * level('chroma');
  viewCtx.drawImage(scene, fringe, 0, viewW, viewH);
  viewCtx.drawImage(scene, -fringe, 0, viewW, viewH);
}
```

Envelope is the slow sine already driving the slip. I did not invent a second clock for the color. Above 0.72, that sine is in the strong part of its breath, and the copies are allowed to draw. The burst opens the same door for the short spike. Alpha 0.045 is where I landed after a higher number doubled the edges and I could see two cubes. On a quiet frame these two copies are not drawn. On a still frame you should almost not know they exist.

STEP 4 — THE FRAME DIED ON A NEGATIVE REMAINDER

With the glass in place I reloaded and got the flat green field again. I assumed the new layer had killed the draw. The cubes were still in the code. The loop had thrown, and a throw means it never asks for the next frame, which I had learned the hard way with the sound import. This time the console said the palette index was −1. I did not know an index could be negative. The list does not have a scene there.

```
let cycle = Number.isFinite(time) ? time % 70 : 0;
if (cycle < 0) cycle += 70;
const i = Math.floor((cycle / 70) * 5) % 5;
```

The color cycle is five scenes over 70 seconds. I expected a remainder to wrap. In other places I have used, −1 mod 70 is 69. In JavaScript, −1 % 70 is −1. The sign stays with the left side. I had to print it to believe it. The first frame can step backward because the timestamp I stored at startup and the timestamp on the first animation callback are both milliseconds, and the callback can come in a little earlier. A negative index reads a scene that is not in the list. Reading its background color threw. The animation stopped. The body green from the first day was the whole picture again. Same symptom as the failed import. Different line. That was the useful part of having been through it once.

The green panel on this page runs both versions, so I can see the mistake without crashing the notes. Time is −1. The raw remainder indexes −1, which is the crash. Adding 70 when the remainder is negative folds it back into the cycle, and that index is a real scene.

Wrapping the remainder fixed the crash. I also stopped the clock from walking backward in the first place, because I did not want to depend on remembering the wrap:

```
let dt = (now - last) / 1000;
if (!Number.isFinite(dt) || dt < 0) dt = 0;
else if (dt > 0.033) dt = 0.033;
time += dt;
```

If dt is not a real number, or if it is negative, I add nothing. Time only moves forward. If a tab stalls and comes back with a huge gap, I cap that gap at 0.033 seconds, which is one frame if I am thinking in thirtieths. I picked it because I was already thinking that way, not because the piece has a tempo. It is a ceiling. The palette and the bars see that ceiling, so they do not jump across a few seconds of life in one tick.

STEP 5 — THE ORDER I LEFT IT IN

This order is the order I ended on, after the crashes, not the order I wrote it down in. Draw the cubes into the clean canvas. Copy that canvas to the stage. If the signal is drifting, redraw the strips, and only then the two faint color copies. On the glass, over the sentence as well: scanlines, grain, any bar that is still alive, then the flicker. Flicker I added late. One sine blinked on a beat, and I could predict it. Two speeds, 17 and 43, added together, wander. The first version of the amount was a strobe and I could not read the sentence. 0.035 is where the field lifts a little and the type survives.

```
const pulse = Math.sin(time * 17) * 0.65 + Math.sin(time * 43) * 0.35;
glass.globalAlpha = Math.abs(pulse) * 0.035 * flicker;
```

I checked, before calling this finished, that the behaviors, the key map, and the sentence were the same objects they were at the end of the sound step. The screen is a pass in front of them. It does not reach back into them. That was the whole constraint, and I had to keep re-checking it, because every one of these effects was easier to build inside the cube than in front of it.

The film is this step and nothing later. A key plays once and dies. The sentence splits on its period. A playhead walks the letters, waits, and comes back to the first phrase. Then Stop, and a second clock that tries to continue is thrown out. Export video saves that pass. The plate under the film is the same clock, live, on whatever you type.

Week 6 ended with a key that moved the grid and, when the sound was in, played a note. Both things happened at the keypress, and then they were over. If I wanted the sentence again I had to type it again. That is a keyboard. I was asked for a performance: the text handled phrase by phrase, the phrase sounding and moving the field, and the whole thing continuing until I stopped it. I did not redraw the cubes. I needed a clock that could ask the cubes the question the keyboard had been asking.

STEP 1 — A PHRASE IS WHERE THE SENTENCE BREATHES

I did not cut the text on a timer. A timer does not know that “Go slow.” is one thought and “Yes.” is the next. I already had marks for that. A period, a question mark, an exclamation, an ellipsis, and Enter. I also had the habit of pressing Start before the last sentence had its period. That unfinished tail had to count, or Start on a live sentence would find nothing to play.

```
const PHRASE_PATTERN = /[^.!?\n…]*[.!?…]+|[^.!?\n…]+/g;
```

The pattern is two choices, tried in that order. The first takes a run of ordinary characters and the punctuation that ends it, and it keeps the punctuation, so “Go slow.” is one phrase and the period stays on it. The second takes a run that has no punctuation yet. I split on line breaks before I run the pattern, because Enter was already a turn in the typing step, and a line break is not inside the character class. Then I trim, and I drop a piece that is only whitespace. “Go slow. Yes.” is two phrases. “Go slow” with no period is one. An empty plate is an empty list, and an empty list is not an error. It is a reason to stay quiet.

STEP 2 — THE PHRASE ASKS THE SAME QUESTION THE KEY DID

I almost wrote a second table from letters to gestures. Then I noticed I already had one. eventForKey, from the typing step, answers for a character: which behavior, which note. A phrase is just the characters in order, each one asked that question again.

```
if (char === ' ' || char === '\t') {
  if (gap) continue;
  gap = true;
  events.push({ behavior: null, sound: null });
  continue;
}
```

A space is a rest. behavior null means the grid is not asked to move, which is what the spacebar already meant. A run of spaces collapses to one rest, so three spaces are one beat and not three empty beats. A comma is still the fallback, the pulse on e4, because the key map already decided that. The phrase does not invent a new instrument. It replays the one I had learned.

STEP 3 — ONE CLOCK, AND IT IS NOT THE DRAWING

The cubes already have a clock: requestAnimationFrame, every frame, forever. I wanted the music to step letter by letter, slower than a frame, and to be able to stop. If I put the stepping inside the drawing loop, Stop would have to grow a flag in the renderer, and the file that paints the field would know about phrases. I kept them apart. The performance only calls trigger(name) and play(sound). Those are the same two calls a keypress made.

```
const STEP_MS = 120;
const TAIL_MS = 420;

function later(ms, fn) {
  clearTimer();
  timer = setTimeout(() => {
    timer = null;
    fn();
  }, ms);
}
```

120 milliseconds is the gap between letters inside a phrase. I arrived at it by listening: faster and a word is a blur, slower and the sentence feels dictated. 420 is the breath after the last letter, before the next phrase starts. later clears the previous timeout before it sets the next, and it keeps a single id. There is one chain. There is not a setInterval, which would keep firing after I had asked it to stop and would drift from the breaths.

STEP 4 — STOP HAS TO MAKE THE OLD CHAIN A STRANGER

clearTimeout is not the whole problem. A callback can be in the middle of deciding to schedule the next letter. If I only clear the timer, that callback still schedules, and the piece continues in the background with nobody holding Start. I needed the old callback to notice that it no longer belongs.

```
function stop() {
  playing = false;
  generation += 1;
  clearTimer();
  silence();
  trigger('reset');
}

function playPhrase(index, gen) {
  if (gen !== generation || !playing) return;
  // ...play this phrase, then later(() => playPhrase(index + 1, gen))
}
```

generation starts at 0. Start adds one and copies the number into gen, and every callback of that performance carries that copy. The first line of the callback compares. Stop adds one to the live number, so a callback born in the old performance sees that its gen is stale and returns before it can schedule anything else. silence releases whatever note is still ringing. trigger('reset') is the same reset Escape already used, so a flip or a scatter does not stay stuck on the field after the music has ended. When the phrase index wraps past the end, I also reset once, before the first phrase plays again. Persistent gestures would otherwise pile up forever. The very first phrase of a Start does not wrap, so the downbeat does not clear the field I am about to play.

STEP 5 — AN EMPTY PLATE DOES NOT START, AND THE BUTTON MUST NOT EAT THE KEYS

Start asks for the phrase list. If the list is empty it returns false, the button stays Start, and nothing is scheduled. I found a smaller bug while testing that. The click left the button focused, and the next keydown went to the button. The sentence did not grow, Start kept finding an empty plate, and I thought the clock was broken. The clock was fine. The keys were not reaching it.

```
button.addEventListener('mousedown', (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();
});
button.addEventListener('click', () => {
  if (player.isPlaying()) player.stop();
  else player.start();
  button.blur();
});
```

preventDefault on mouse-down stops the button from taking focus. blur on the click lets go of it if it has it. The key listener also returns immediately when the target is the Start button, or when the target is inside the dials, which come later. A held modifier, or a composition session for another writing system, is ignored, the same as in the typing step. The page hides on leaving, and that calls stop, so a performance does not continue in a tab I have already left.

At the end of this step the sentence can play itself, and play itself again, and Stop is actually quiet. On this page the clock photographs the plate when you press Start, so you can watch one pass without the sentence sliding out from under it. In the piece, the first version did not photograph anything. It asked the plate again at every phrase. I did not hear why that mattered until the dials had a button and the words still changed the loop. That is page 4. The sound, for now, is still the oscillator from week 6. The next step is the instrument I was asked to learn, and the first thing it did was try to open the speakers before I had clicked.

The film is this step. First the page is only green, because the import ran before any picture. Then the cubes are back, and Start is what loads the script. Then three waves: the sine carrier, the square modulator at three times the pitch, and the tone they make when one pushes the other. Export video records those tones. The button under the film is a stand-in you can hear here. The piece uses Tone.FMSynth, and only after Start.

The loop from the previous step could already play the week 6 oscillator. I was pointed at an FM synth, the Tone.js example with a square modulator. FM is frequency modulation. One oscillator, the modulator, moves the pitch of another, the carrier, so fast that you do not hear a vibrato. You hear new harmonics. How far it pushes is the modulation index. I wanted that inside the phrase clock I had just made. I did not want a second clock. Tone has a Transport that schedules its own bars. Using it would mean two conductors. The phrase timer stays the conductor. Tone only has to be the instrument.

STEP 1 — THE IMPORT OPENED THE SPEAKERS BY EXISTING

I started the way the example starts, by importing the library. The build I imported is a module, and at the top of that module it creates the audio context, on the line that exports it. Importing the file runs that line. The page had not been clicked. I had decided, because of the browser’s autoplay rule and because of the week 6 bug, that the context would exist only after a gesture. This import broke that before my own code ran. The same file also imports other packages by their bare names, standardized-audio-context and tslib. A browser cannot find a bare name without a bundler, and I do not have one. Week 6 had already taught me what a failed import looks like: the green field, and no cubes, because the module graph died before the first frame.

```
// the ESM build, which I did not keep
export const context = getContext();
```

getContext is called while the file is being evaluated. That is too early. I stopped importing that build. The picture loads with no Tone on the page at all. I checked: before Start there is no window.Tone, and no audio context from this piece.

STEP 2 — THE SCRIPT WAITS FOR THE CLICK

There is a single-file build of the same library, the UMD build. It does not import anything by a bare name. When the script runs, it attaches the library to window.Tone, and that run is also the moment it creates the context. So I must not put the script in the html. I create the tag from the Start click.

```
function loadTone() {
  if (tone) return Promise.resolve(tone);
  const script = document.createElement('script');
  script.src = new URL('../vendor/Tone.js', import.meta.url);
  script.onload = () => { tone = window.Tone; };
  document.head.appendChild(script);
}

export function beginAudio() {
  return loadTone().then((Tone) => Tone.start());
}
```

import.meta.url is the address of the sound file. The script points at Tone beside the page, so the piece does not fetch the library from the network. I copied it in, and I copied its MIT license with it. onload is the moment window.Tone exists. Tone.start resumes the context. Start’s click is the gesture, and the file is on this machine, so the resume still happens inside that gesture. The second Start finds tone already set and does not insert a second script. It only resumes.

The UMD build prints a banner when it evaluates. There is a flag, window.TONE_SILENCE_LOGGING, and the build reads it at that moment. I set it in the html, before the module, so the flag is already true when the script I inject later finally runs. Setting it inside beginAudio would be too late. The banner would already have printed.

STEP 3 — ONE VOICE THAT CAN HOLD A WORD

An FMSynth plays one note. A word is several notes, and the earlier ones are still dying while the next one starts. PolySynth is a small pool of the same voice. I asked it for FMSynth, and I gave it the patch from the example, trimmed to the numbers I was willing to live with.

```
new Tone.PolySynth({
  voice: Tone.FMSynth,
  maxPolyphony: 8,
  options: {
    harmonicity: 3,
    modulationIndex: 12.2,
    modulation: { type: 'square' },
    envelope: { attack: 0.01, decay: 0.2, sustain: 0.4, release: 0.4 },
    modulationEnvelope: { attack: 0.2, decay: 0.01, sustain: 0.2, release: 0.2 },
  },
});
```

harmonicity 3 means the modulator’s frequency is three times the carrier’s. That ratio is what makes the tone feel like the example, a particular set of overtones, rather than a generic buzz. modulationIndex 12.2 is how hard the modulator pushes. I started from the example’s 12.22 and kept one decimal. The modulator’s shape is a square, which is brighter than a sine before the index even rises. The envelope is the carrier’s loudness: attack 0.01 seconds so the note is there, decay 0.2, sustain at 0.4 of the peak so it does not vanish while the key is notionally held, release 0.4 so it leaves. The modulation envelope is how the index itself blooms and dies. Its attack is slower, 0.2, so the brightness arrives a little after the note. I connect the synth to a Gain, and the Gain to the speakers. The gain starts at 0.7 × 0.4, which is 0.28. The 0.4 is a ceiling I chose so that later, when more than one voice exists, the sum still has somewhere to go.

STEP 4 — THE LETTER IS STILL THE PITCH

I did not throw away the row from week 6. c3, d3, f3, and on through d5, no E and no B, the letter choosing the index. The behavior still chooses the duration and the gain. What changed is the call that makes the sound.

```
voice.triggerAttackRelease(pitch, length, voice.now(), velocity);
```

triggerAttackRelease starts the note and schedules the moment it lets go. pitch is the letter’s note, or the list of notes when Enter asks for the chord c3 and g3. length is the duration from the behavior, and it has to be greater than zero, because Tone refuses a zero-length note. velocity is the behavior’s gain scaled and clamped between 0.02 and 1, so a scatter, which was already quiet, stays quieter than a swell. voice.now() is the audio clock plus a small lookahead. I tried the raw current time first. A short note’s release landed inside the window Tone treats as already due, and the release fired immediately. The note had no body. now() puts the attack on the edge of that window, so I hear it at once, and the release is still ahead of the clock. Stop calls releaseAll(voice.now()) on the same clock, which can still reach a note that is ringing. A release scheduled in the past cannot be caught, because it has already happened.

STEP 5 — THE OLD SYNTH LEAVES THE PROGRAM AND STAYS IN THE FOLDER

Week 6’s synth was still imported. If I left the import, every phrase would be answered twice, by two instruments that do not know about each other. I removed the import, and I removed the import map that gave the package its local file. The old sound library is still on disk. Nothing points at it. One context. One kind of voice. The phrase clock from the previous step is unchanged: it still calls play(sound) once per letter. Only the inside of play has become this FM voice.

At the end of this step a phrase is an FM tone, and the speakers open on Start and go quiet on Stop. The numbers of the patch are still buried in the file. The next step is to put four of them where a hand can reach them, without letting the hand rewrite the music by accident.

The film is two columns. The plate is what I am writing. Hearing is what the clock is playing. They start as the same phrase. Then I type Yes, and hearing changes while the button still says Add. That is the mistake. At the end Add does fire, and the one voice is replaced: pitch 0 is crossed out and +7 is all that remains. The plate under the film lets you watch the same early clock.

The dials could be moved without touching the voice. I still needed a way to say: this, now. I put a button under them and named it Add. I chose that word because I thought I was adding a decision to the performance. What the button actually did, the first time, was replace the sound I was already hearing. And the sentence, which I had not put behind the button at all, kept rewriting the loop on its own.

STEP 1 — THE HAND WRITES A DRAFT

Four inputs, four numbers. I read them only when the button is pressed. Until then they are a draft, the same way a sentence on the plate is a draft until I decide it is part of the piece. Or so I thought.

```
function readDraft() {
  return {
    pitch: Number(pitchInput.value),
    volume: Number(volumeInput.value),
    harmonics: Number(harmonicsInput.value),
    length: Number(lengthInput.value),
  };
}
```

Number parses the slider’s string. The ranges from the previous step are still on the inputs, so a draft is already inside the limits: pitch between −12 and 12, volume between 0 and 1, harmonics between 0 and 20, length between 0.5 and 2. Moving a slider still only changes its readout. Add is the first moment those four numbers are allowed to mean something.

STEP 2 — I WAITED FOR THE PHRASE, AND THEN I REPLACED IT

I did not want the tone to jump in the middle of a word. So the first Add, if the loop was running, stored the draft and changed the button to Added. The phrase clock, at the top of the next phrase, applied the draft to the one FM voice. Then the button said Add again. If nothing was playing, it applied immediately, because there was no phrase to wait for.

```
function commitMix() {
  setPerformanceControls(pendingMix);
  pendingMix = null;
}
```

setPerformanceControls wrote pitch, volume, harmonics, and length onto the single synth, ramping the ones that can glide. The voice I had been listening to was gone. The new numbers were the whole piece. I called that a commit. Listening to it, I noticed I had not added anything. I had swapped. The old brightness did not continue under the new one. There was only ever one voice, so a new setting had nowhere to go except onto that voice.

STEP 3 — THE SENTENCE WAS NEVER BEHIND THE BUTTON

Then I did the thing I actually do when I am playing: I typed more words. I had not pressed Add. The next phrase was different. New letters, new pitches, the grid answering to characters I was still writing. The dials had waited. The sentence had not. I went looking for the line, and it was the clock asking for the plate again every time a phrase began.

```
function playPhrase(index, gen) {
  if (gen !== generation || !playing) return;
  const phrases = splitPhrases(getText() || '');
  // the next phrase is whatever the plate says right now
}
```

getText is the plate. The plate is the string I am editing. So the score and the draft were the same string. The moment the clock finished a phrase, it split the plate again and played whatever was there, including the word I had just typed. That is why adding words changed the sound without Add. The button was guarding the dials. Nothing was guarding the sentence. I had built a commit for the tone and left the score live, and the score is also a sound. Every letter is a pitch.

What I wanted, once I heard it, was simpler and stricter. The loop that is already going should keep going, the same words, the same tone. When I press Add, the new sound should arrive with the old one, both of them, not one in place of the other. The next page is that.

The film is the stack, not another caption on the same sentence. Layer 1 walks “Go slow.” at pitch 0. The plate then grows, and layer 1 does not. Add brings “Yes.” in at +7, and both keep walking. A second Add brings “Hi” in, and the first two are still there. Export video records the tones on top of each other. The plate under the film is the same rule, live.

A layer is a sentence that has been accepted, the dials that belonged to it at that moment, and its own FM voice. The performance is a list of those. They share the one clock from the loop page. They do not share a synth. Once I had the list, the bug on the previous page had a place to go: the plate can keep changing, because the list is no longer the plate.

STEP 1 — START TAKES A PICTURE

On Start, if the list is empty, I split the plate and I read the dials, and both go onto the first layer. A string called claim remembers exactly the text I accepted. After that the clock plays the phrases stored on the layer. It does not call getText again. I can keep typing. The plate grows. The first layer’s sentence is the sentence from the click.

```
if (score.length === 0 && !claim) {
  seedText = getText() || '';
  seedMix = getMix();
  seedPhrases = takePhrases(seedText);
  if (!seedPhrases) return false;
}
```

An empty plate still refuses to start. There is nothing to photograph. If I Stop and Start again, the list is not empty, so this block does not run. Start does not look at the words I typed while I was stopped. It rewinds the layers I already accepted and plays them from the first letter. The new words are still only on the plate, waiting for Add.

STEP 2 — ADD KEEPS WHAT IS NEW

```
if (claim && source.startsWith(claim)) {
  if (source.length === claim.length) return splitPhrases(source);
  const extra = splitPhrases(source.slice(claim.length));
  if (!extra.length) return null;
  claim = source;
  return extra;
}
```

startsWith is the test for “I added onto the end.” slice takes only the tail. “Go slow.” is already looping. I type “ Yes.” and press Add. The new layer is Yes. The first layer is still Go slow. Both play. If the tail is only spaces, splitPhrases drops it, and Add does nothing, because I did not actually add a word. If I have not typed anything and I have only moved a dial, the plate is the same length as claim, so the new layer is the whole sentence again, with the new dials. I hear the same words twice, in two tones. That is the case the button was originally trying to be, and it only works once the old tone is allowed to remain. If I rewrote the beginning, the plate no longer starts with claim, and the new layer is the whole plate, still beside the old layer. I do not go back and edit a voice that is already sounding. claim then becomes the plate I just accepted, so the next Add is only the words after that.

STEP 3 — EACH LAYER IS BORN WITH ITS TONE

The single synth could not hold two indexes at once. A modulation index is a property of the voice, not of the note. So Add builds another voice.

```
const voice = new tone.PolySynth({
  voice: tone.FMSynth,
  maxPolyphony: 8,
  options: voiceSettings(tuned),
});
const output = new tone.Gain(tuned.volume * 0.4);
voice.connect(output);
output.toDestination();
```

voiceSettings copies the draft into the patch: detune is pitch × 100, modulationIndex is the harmonics, decay and release are 0.2 and 0.4 times the length. The Gain is that layer’s volume times the same 0.4 ceiling. The id of this voice goes onto the layer, and playSound(params, layerId) looks it up. A later Add does not ramp the first voice. There is nothing to ramp. The first voice’s numbers were copied when it was born, and they stay there. Six is the cap. A seventh Add returns null and the score does not grow. Each voice is already two oscillators, a carrier and a modulator, and the gains add in the air. I would rather the button do nothing than let a seventh voice turn the sum into distortion I did not choose.

STEP 4 — THE FIELD FOLLOWS THE FIRST SENTENCE

```
drivesVisuals: !score.some((item) => item.drivesVisuals)
```

Only the first layer calls trigger. Its letters still move the cubes, and when its own phrases wrap it calls reset, the same reset as the single loop, so the persistent gestures do not pile up. A new layer does not get that flag. If it did, a two-letter overdub would clear the field several times inside one pass of the original sentence, and the picture would belong to the shortest layer. The new words are heard. The picture stays with the sentence I started. The drawing file is still untouched. The performance only calls the same trigger it has always called.

STEP 5 — ONE CLOCK, SEVERAL NEXT TIMES

Each layer carries a nextTime, a phrase index, and a letter index. The tick fires every layer whose time has arrived, then sleeps until the soonest next letter. Inside a phrase the gap is still 120 milliseconds. After a phrase the breath is still 420. A short layer loops its own words faster than a long one. They are not forced onto the same bar. They are in the same room.

```
if (timer === null) {
  clock = 0;
  prime(layer, 0);
  tick(generation);
  return;
}
prime(layer, clock);
```

If the loop is already running, the new layer is primed at the clock’s current time, so it speaks on the next tick. The other layers are not rewound. If nothing is running, the layer waits in the list until Start. There is still one timer id. Stop clears it and increments generation, so a callback from the old pass cannot schedule the next letter of any layer.

STEP 6 — STOP KEEPS THE STACK, ESCAPE THROWS IT AWAY

```
function stop() {
  playing = false;
  generation += 1;
  clearTimer();
  silence();
  trigger('reset');
}

function reset() {
  stop();
  epoch += 1;
  score.length = 0;
  claim = '';
  disposeLayers();
}
```

silence is releaseAll on every voice, at each voice’s now(), the same catchable clock as the single synth. The layers stay. Start plays them again from the first letter. The words typed after the last Add are still only written.

Escape is the reset from the typing step. It stops, empties the list, forgets claim, and disposes the voices. The audio context stays, so the next Start does not have to fetch Tone again. The performance is gone, which is what Escape meant when all it did was clear the sentence. epoch is a second counter, for the gap while Tone is still loading. Start and Add are promises. If I press Escape during that gap, epoch moves, and the promise that resolves afterward sees that it is late and does not build a voice onto a piece I have already cleared.

At the end of this step I can write, listen, move a dial, and change my mind. The loop I already trusted keeps going. Add brings the new sound in with it. The field is the field from week 6. The sentence is finally a score I can keep, and a score I can add to.
